# @corpus current
# ONE NAME IN PARENTHESES, TWO READINGS, and neither is inside the group.
#
#   `twice: i64 = (t)` over an indented line is a RELATION and `t` is its
#   parameter — the shape the `fun` retirement produces every time it rewrites
#   `fun(t)`.
#   `else(flag)` over an indented line is an ELSE-IF and `flag` is its
#   condition.
#
# The group is identical; what separates them is the keyword before it and the
# offside line beneath it. `else(gate(n))` is the same else-if with an
# APPLICATION under the name, which a recognizer counting only DEPTH-1 tokens
# cannot see — it reads `(name)` and takes the else's own block as a lambda
# body. That is how six tracked files stopped parsing in one afternoon:
# lib/slices.id, lib/sort.id, lib/net/url.id, scripts/runtime_bench.id, and the
# two in-tree LSP files retired on 2026-08-17.
#
# CHECKED BY VALUE, and it discriminates in BOTH directions, which is the only
# reason the number proves anything:
#
#   * Read `(t)` as a grouped expression rather than a parameter list and
#     `twice` binds the VALUE of `t` while `t + t` becomes a separate top-level
#     statement — `twice(3)` then has nothing to apply. Measured before the fix:
#     `idol fmt` reprinted this relation as `twice: i64 = t` on one line and
#     `t + t` on the next, and `idol check` called that clean.
#   * Read either `else(…)` as a relation header and the file never closes its
#     parenthesis at all.
#
# No output: the process exit status is the observable, so no string literal
# appears here and nothing about this file runs through interpolation.
# expect: 11

twice: i64 = (t)
  t + t

gate: i64 = (v: i64)
  v

pick: i64 = (n: i64, flag: i64)
  if n > 5
    1
  else(gate(n))
    twice(2)
  else(flag)
    3
  else
    9

main: i64 = ()
  pick(1, 0) + pick(9, 0) + twice(3)
